 ## Overview
-Densification Strategy manages the dynamic lifecycle of 3D Gaussian primitives during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by iteratively splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics [Parent]. It solves the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space, which static initialization methods cannot address [13466-densification-and-splitting.md]. What sets this family apart from static initialization or single-stage optimization is the feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive set topology throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [13466-densification-and-splitting.md].
+Densification strategies manage the topology of the 3D Gaussian primitive set $\mathcal{G}$ during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by dynamically adding, splitting, cloning, or removing primitives [Parent]. This family addresses the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space, which static initialization from sparse Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points cannot resolve [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization or fixed-parameter methods is the iterative, feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive population throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All densification strategies in this family operate on a set of 3D Gaussian primitives $\mathcal{G} = \{g_1, \dots, g_N\}$, where each $g_k$ is defined by a mean position $\mu_k \in \mathbb{R}^3$, a covariance matrix $\Sigma_k$ (parameterized by scale $s_k$ and rotation $q_k$), opacity $\alpha_k$, and spherical harmonic coefficients $\mathbf{c}_k$ [S8, unofficial].
