 ## Background
-The concept of densification in 3D Gaussian Splatting originates from the original 3DGS paper [4] (cited in [S1]), which introduced the idea of splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D positional gradients to handle complex geometry. Subsequent developments have diversified the strategy to address specific challenges such as dynamic scenes ([GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md)), memory efficiency ([Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md)), and active reconstruction ([Termination entropy criterion](#termination-entropy-criterion)). The field has evolved from simple gradient-based heuristics to more sophisticated methods involving MCMC sampling ([3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md)), neural confidence scores ([VS-Splat Voxel Selection](9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md)), and multi-stage progressive training ([Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md)).
+The concept of densification in 3D Gaussian Splatting originates from the original 3DGS paper [Parent], which introduced the idea of splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D positional gradients to handle under-reconstructed regions. Subsequent developments have expanded this into a diverse family of strategies addressing specific challenges such as dynamic scenes ([GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md)), memory efficiency ([Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md)), and automated hyperparameter tuning ([TangoGS](10180-tangogs.md)). The field continues to evolve with methods integrating MCMC sampling ([3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md)), neural refinement ([Adaptive Gaussian Refinement](11448-adaptive-gaussian-refinement.md)), and multi-stage training ([Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md)).
 
